# Expected ERROR-class log lines for a round that runs chaos/run.sh.
#
# Pass this to analyze-evidence.sh ONLY for a round that actually injected a fault,
# and only after confirming chaos-events.csv recorded a sigkill. It is not a general
# baseline allowlist.
#
# Format: one extended-regex per line; '#' comments and blank lines ignored.
# Same format as the sibling <round>.allowed-errors files.
#
# ── The one entry, and why ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `chaos/run.sh` scenario `kill-9-mid-forge` SIGKILLs the SOLE forger deliberately,
# with no warning and no chance to finish what it was doing. A forger killed between
# TraceForgedBlock and TraceAdoptedBlock can legitimately restart, replay, resume
# forging, and find one of its blocks beaten to the tip — which it reports as
# TraceDidntAdoptBlock and stores as a fork.
#
# That is the CORRECT and SAFE outcome: the node kept the better chain instead of
# adopting its own late block. cardano-node logs the same trace at the same severity,
# so dugite is not mis-levelling it. Measured 2026-08-07 in a release gate: SIGKILL at
# 09:58:45 with tip block 633 (slot 1228), restart at 09:58:47 which correctly refused
# the volatile-tip snapshot and replayed from genesis, then at slot 1259 block 641 was
# forged and not adopted. Zero invalid-block events, cardano-bp 0 ERROR, and every
# verify.sh predicate green including 24/24 tip parity.
#
# Whether the kill lands in that window is a coin flip — the immediately preceding
# gate run produced 0 ERROR from the identical suite. Failing the baseline round on
# that is not a signal, it is a dice roll, and it is why this entry exists.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT COVER, deliberately: analyze-evidence.sh applies no allowlist to
# the invalid-block check. TraceForgedInvalidBlock / AddBlockValidation.InvalidBlock /
# Forge.Loop.ForgedInvalidBlock remain CRITICAL and unconditional, because a
# Haskell-rejected dugite block is the failure this harness exists to catch. Do not
# widen this file to a bare "forge" or "ERROR" pattern; that would swallow exactly
# that class.
TraceDidntAdoptBlock
